course: dictionary
lesson: 15002
title: Reseal: closing it again
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To reseal something means to close or seal it again after it has been opened. The prefix re- means again, so reseal means seal again. «Mum opened the crisp packet, ate a few, and resealed it.» She sealed it again after opening it.
explain: Reseal can also mean to put a new seal or protective coat on something. «Dad resealed the edges of the bath to keep the water in.» He added fresh sealant, so the bath is protected again. Floors and roads can be resealed too.
explain: A resealable bag has a zip or strip that lets you close it again and again. «The resealable bag kept the grapes fresh all week.» Resealable is an adjective, so it describes the bag.
fact: Reseal is a verb: reseal, reseals, resealed, resealing.
fact: Resealable is an adjective: «a resealable pack» is one you can close again.
fact: The prefix re- means again, so resealing means sealing again.
fact: Resealing a container helps stop air and germs getting in.
fact: «Reseal the bag» means close the bag again after you have opened it.
exercise: «Please reseal the bag after you take out a biscuit.» What should you do to the bag? | *Close it again | Open it wider | Put it on the floor | Throw it in the bin
exercise: «The resealable pack kept the crisps crunchy.» What does resealable describe? | A pack made of gold | A pack that is very heavy | *A pack you can close again | A pack that is always open
exercise: What does the prefix re- mean in reseal? | Before | *Again | Under | Away from
test: Dad resealed the edges of the bath. What did he do? | *Put a new protective coat on the edges | Filled the bath with hot water | Painted the walls | Took the bath away
test: Why do scientists reseal a container? | To let more air in | To make it heavier | To make it louder | *To stop air and germs getting in
exam: Which word in «Ella resealed the packet before school» is the verb? | packet | Ella | before | *resealed
